Building a Business Directory Listing app using Firebase, Express.js, and Flutter

Step 1: Set up the Firebase project

1. Go to the Firebase Console (https://console.firebase.google.com/) and sign in with your Google account.
2. Click on the "Add project" button and enter your project name, enable Google Analytics if you want, and create your project.
3. After your project is created, select "Database" in the left-hand menu and click "Create database" for Firestore (use either test mode or production mode, depending on your preference).
4. Set up authentication for your app:
   a. In the Firebase Console, click "Authentication" in the left-hand menu.
   b. Click on "Set up sign-in method" and enable your preferred sign-in methods for your users and business owners (email/password, Google, Facebook, etc.).

Step 2: Set up Express.js backend

1. Install Node.js (https://nodejs.org/en/download/) on your local machine, if you haven't already.
2. Next, set up an Express.js project by running the following commands:
```
$ mkdir business-directory-backend
$ cd business-directory-backend
$ npm init
$ npm install express firebase-admin body-parser cors
$ touch index.js
```
3. Open your 'index.js' file and add the following code to initialize your Firebase Admin SDK and Express.js app:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const admin = require('firebase-admin');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');
const cors = require('cors');

const app = express();
app.use(cors());
app.use(bodyParser.json());

var serviceAccount = require('./path/to/your/firebase-adminsdk-key.json');

admin.initializeApp({
  credential: admin.credential.cert(serviceAccount)
});

const db = admin.firestore();

// Add business
app.post('/business', async (req, res) => {
  // TODO: Implement adding a new business to Firestore
});

// Get businesses
app.get('/businesses', async (req, res) => {
  // TODO: Implement getting businesses from Firestore
});

// Add review
app.post('/review', async (req, res) => {
  // TODO: Implement adding a review to a business
});

// Set up server
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 8000;
app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```
4. Implement the necessary endpoints to create businesses, get businesses, add reviews, etc.

Step 3: Set up the Flutter frontend

1. Install Flutter (https://flutter.dev/docs/get-started/install) and set up your development environment (e.g., Android Studio, VS Code, etc.).
2. Create a new Flutter project:
```
$ flutter create  business_directory_app
$ cd business_directory_app
```
3. Add Firebase and other required packages to your 'pubspec.yaml' file:
```
dependencies:
  flutter:
    sdk: flutter
  firebase_core: ^1.10.4
  firebase_auth: ^3.3.5
  cloud_firestore: ^3.1.8
  http: ^0.13.3
```
4. Set up Firebase for your Flutter app (https://firebase.google.com/docs/flutter/setup).

Step 4: Implement core features

1. Implement authentication in your Flutter app (sign-up, sign-in, sign-out).
   a. Create screens for sign-up, sign-in, and the main app.
   b. Implement sign-up and sign-in logic.
2. Implement the logic to add and display businesses.
   a. Create a screen for adding a new business.
   b. Implement logic to send a new business to the backend.
   c. Create a screen to display businesses based on location, category, and keywords.
3. Implement reviewing and rating features.
   a. Create a screen for users to review and rate businesses.
   b. Implement logic to send and receive reviews from the backend.
4. Implement bookmarking and managing businesses.
   a. Create a way for users to bookmark their favorite places.
   b. Implement screens for business owners to edit or claim businesses.

Step 5: Deploy your app

1. Deploy your Express.js backend on a hosting service of your choice (e.g., Heroku, Google Cloud, AWS, etc.).
2. Update your Flutter app to use the deployed backend API instead of the local one.
3. Test your app on Android and iOS, make sure everything is working as expected.
